-- title:
--   Good points of the integral model read a unique place (q=2)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ denotes the algebraic closure of $\mathbb Q$ used in the project, and for a field $K$ the Laurent series field $K((X))$ carries the intermediate fields
--   $$\mathcal M_{M'}:=\mathtt{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'\subseteq \overline{\mathbb Q}((X)),\qquad \mathcal F:=\mathtt{fieldBar}\,q\,M'=\mathtt{xHFunctionFieldBar}\,(q^2M')\,(\mathtt{levelH}\,q\,M'),$$
--   the first the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-base change (adjunction of the coefficientwise images) of the full level-$M'$ modular function field, the second the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-base change of the function field of level $\Gamma_H(q^2M')$, where $\mathtt{levelH}\,q\,M'$ is the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$. For a field $k$ one writes $\mathcal M^k_{M'}:=\mathtt{modularFunctionFieldC}\,k\,M'$, the $k$-subfield of $k((X))$ generated by the two series $\mathtt{jqModC}\,k$ and $\mathtt{jqNModC}\,k\,M'$, and $\mathcal F^k:=\mathtt{xHFunctionFieldC}\,k\,(q^2M')\,(\mathtt{levelH}\,q\,M')$, the $k$-subfield generated by the ratios of integral $q$-expansions of forms of level $\Gamma_H(q^2M')$. A place of $F$ over $K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$ and with principal ideals; $P.\mathrm{ord}$ is minus the logarithm of its adic valuation, $P$ is rational when $K$ surjects onto its residue field, and $P.\mathrm{evalAt}$ is the induced $K$-valued evaluation.
--
--   Data. A prime $q$ with $q=2$; a nonzero natural number $M'$ with $q\nmid M'$; a valuation subring $A\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q$ a nonunit of $A$ (the predicate $\mathtt{LiesOverPrime}$), with residue field $k:=\mathtt{ResidueField}\,A$; a finite set $W$ of places of $\mathcal M^k_{M'}$ over $k$ whose members are, by $hW$, exactly the supersingular places, i.e. the rational places $w$ that are affine geometric and whose value $w.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathtt{jGeomGen}\,k\,M')$ lies in $\mathtt{ssJSet}\,q\,k$ (the set of $j$-values all of whose elliptic curves have no nonzero $q$-torsion point); an inclusion $hle:\mathcal M_{M'}\le\mathcal F$; a constant reduction $R_0$ of $A$ from $\mathcal M_{M'}$ to $\mathcal M^k_{M'}$, that is, a valuation subring $R_0.\mathtt{integers}$ of $\mathcal M_{M'}$ with a surjective residue map to $\mathcal M^k_{M'}$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal, compatible with $A\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and its residue map, together with a place map preserving degrees and divisor push-forward; the hypothesis $hR_0$ that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image lies in $\mathcal M_{M'}$ that image lies in $R_0.\mathtt{integers}$ and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $k$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$; an index $\zeta$ in $\mathtt{Idx}\,q$, i.e. a primitive $q$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; families of valuation subrings $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ of $\mathcal F$ indexed by the projective line $\mathbb P^1(\mathbb Z/q)$ and $O_{\mathrm{SS}}$ of $\mathcal F$ indexed by $W$.
--
--   Hypotheses on the Igusa family (four clauses): $hIg\_inf$ describes $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\infty)$, where $\infty=\mathtt{lineInfty}\,q$ is the class of $(1,0)$, as the Gauss ring: $f\in O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\infty)$ if and only if there are Laurent series $x,y$ over $A$ with nonzero coefficientwise reduction of $y$ and $f\cdot\bar y=\bar x$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}((X))$; $hIg$ states that every line $\ell$ is $\mathtt{redQ}\,q\,\gamma\cdot\infty$ for some $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ with $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\ell)$ the pullback of $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\infty)$ along $\mathtt{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta\,\gamma$; $hIg\_inj$ that $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ is injective; $hIg\_perm$ that for every index $\zeta'$ and every $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ pullback along $\mathtt{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta'\,\gamma$ permutes the family.
--
--   Hypotheses on the supersingular family (three clauses): $hSS\_A$, that for $x\in\overline{\mathbb Q}$ the image of $x$ lies in $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ exactly when $x\in A$; $hSS\_over$, that for $s\in W$ and $f\in R_0.\mathtt{integers}$ which is regular wherever the $j$-element (the image in $\mathcal M_{M'}$ of $\mathtt{coeffEmb}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}\,\mathtt{jq}$) is, in the sense that $0\le P.\mathrm{ord}(j)$ implies $0\le P.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ for every place $P$ of $\mathcal M_{M'}$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, the image of $f$ in $\mathcal F$ lies in $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ and, for every $a\in A$ whose residue equals $s.\mathrm{evalAt}(R_0.\mathrm{residue}\,f)$, the difference of that image and the image of $a$ lies in the maximal ideal of $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$; $hSS\_fix$, that each $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ is invariant under pullback along $\mathtt{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta'\,\gamma$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$; and $hSS\_tr$, that each $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$ contains an element $t$ such that $t$ minus the image of any $a\in A$ is a unit of $O_{\mathrm{SS}}(s)$.
--
--   The reduction data at $\infty$: a regular prolongation $R$ of $A$ from $\mathcal F$ to $\mathcal F^k$ (a valuation subring with surjective residue map onto $\mathcal F^k$, kernel the maximal ideal, compatible with $A$), with $hR:R.\mathtt{integers}=O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\infty)$, and $hR_0O$, saying that $f\in R_0.\mathtt{integers}$ if and only if its image in $\mathcal F$ lies in $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\infty)$.
--
--   Arithmetic input. An element $\pi\in A$ with $\pi^{q^2-1}=q$; an intermediate field $k_0$ of $\mathbb Q\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\pi_0\in k_0$ lying in $A$ such that $A\cap k_0$ is a henselian discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$ and algebraically closed residue field, together with $h\kappa$: every element of $A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element of $k_0\cap A$. An auxiliary prime $\ell\ge3$ with $\ell\ne q$ and $\ell\nmid M'$; an element $\zeta_0\in k_0$ which is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity; an element $\varpi_t\in k_0\cap A$ with $\varpi_t^{q^2-1}=q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$. Finally a finite extension $K_1$ of $k_0$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and a valuation subring $A_1$ of $K_1$ cutting out $A$, i.e. $x\in A_1$ iff $x\in A$, which is a henselian discrete valuation ring.
--
--   The assertion. Give $\mathcal F$ the $k_0$-algebra structure obtained from $k_0\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}\to\mathcal F$. Then for every intermediate field $F_0$ of $k_0\subseteq\mathcal F$ satisfying: (a) $k_0$ adjoined with the range of $\overline{\mathbb Q}\to\mathcal F$, joined with $F_0$, is all of $\mathcal F$; (b) $F_0$ is stable under every $\mathtt{levelAutBar}\,q\,M'\,\zeta'\,\gamma$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$; (c) for every finite extension $K'$ of $k_0$ in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, every $m$, every $c:\mathrm{Fin}\,m\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ linearly independent over $K'$ and every $a:\mathrm{Fin}\,m\to\mathcal F$ with all $a_i$ in $T':=k_0(\text{image of }K')\sqcup F_0$, the relation $\sum_i c_i\,a_i=0$ forces all $a_i=0$; (d) every element of $\mathcal F$ whose underlying Laurent series lies in the range of $\mathtt{coeffEmb}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}$ belongs to $F_0$ — and, writing $T_1:=k_0(\text{image of }K_1)\sqcup F_0$, for every $A_1$-algebra structure on $T_1$ whose structure map agrees with $A_1\subseteq K_1\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}\to\mathcal F$, every $j_1\in T_1$ whose image in $\mathcal F$ is the inclusion along $hle$ of the $j$-element $\mathtt{coeffEmb}\,\overline{\mathbb Q}\,\mathtt{jq}$, with $j_1\ne0$, and every finite set $N_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ of places of $\mathcal F^k$ over $k$ for which there exists a ring homomorphism $\jmath:\mathcal M^k_{M'}\to\mathcal F^k$ such that each $f\in R_0.\mathtt{integers}$ has its image in $\mathcal F$ inside $R.\mathtt{integers}$ with $R$-residue $\jmath(R_0.\mathrm{residue}\,f)$, and such that $Q\in N_{\mathrm{Ig}}$ if and only if there is $s\in W$ with $g$ in the valuation subring of $s$ exactly when $\jmath(g)$ lies in that of $Q$, the following holds for the two-chart integral model
--   $$\mathfrak X:=\mathtt{AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel}\,A_1\,T_1\,j_1,$$
--   the pushout gluing $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\mathtt{chartAlgFin}$ (the elements of $T_1$ integral over $A_1[j_1]$) and $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\mathtt{chartAlgInf}$ (those integral over $A_1[j_1^{-1}]$) along the middle chart, with canonical morphisms $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}},\iota_{\mathrm{Inf}}$ and structure morphism $\mathtt{toBase}$ to $\mathrm{Spec}\,A_1$.
--
--   Five predicates are introduced. For a point $x$ of $\mathfrak X$ and $f\in T_1$: $\mathtt{InStalk}\,x\,f$ says that for every point $y$ of either chart mapping to $x$ there are $g,h$ in the corresponding chart algebra with $h\notin y$ and $f\,h=g$ in $T_1$; $\mathtt{InMax}\,x\,f$ says the same with in addition $g\in y$. For a place $P$ of $\mathcal F$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $\mathtt{Centred}\,P\,x$ says that $P$ is rational and that every $f$ with $\mathtt{InStalk}\,x\,f$ lies in the valuation subring of $P$, has $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f)\in A$, and satisfies: the $A$-valuation of $P.\mathrm{evalAt}(f)$ is $<1$ if and only if $\mathtt{InMax}\,x\,f$. The predicate $\mathtt{GoodPt}\,x$ consists of five clauses: $\mathtt{toBase}$ sends $x$ to the closed point of $\mathrm{Spec}\,A_1$; $x$ specialises only to itself; for every point $y$ of the finite chart over $x$, every chart element whose image in $\mathcal F$ is a nonunit of $R.\mathtt{integers}$ lies in $y$; the same for the infinite chart; and for every point $y$ of the finite chart over $x$, every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the finite chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(\mathtt{jChartFin})$ of $j_1$ does not lie in $\mathtt{ssJSet}\,q\,\Omega$. The predicate $\mathtt{Reads}\,x\,Q$, for $Q$ a place of $\mathcal F^k$ over $k$, says that every $f$ with $\mathtt{InStalk}\,x\,f$ has its image in $R.\mathtt{integers}$, with $R$-residue in the valuation subring of $Q$, and that this residue is a nonunit there if and only if $\mathtt{InMax}\,x\,f$. Finally $\mathtt{OffBranch}\,x$ says that for every line $\ell\ne\infty$ and every point $y$ of either chart over $x$ there is a chart element, not in $y$, whose image in $\mathcal F$ is a nonunit of $O_{\mathrm{Ig}}(\ell)$.
--
--   The conclusion is a single statement: for every point $x$ of $\mathfrak X$ with $\mathtt{GoodPt}\,x$, and all places $Q,Q'$ of $\mathcal F^k$ over $k$, if $\mathtt{Reads}\,x\,Q$ and $\mathtt{Reads}\,x\,Q'$ then $Q=Q'$. The predicates $\mathtt{Centred}$ and $\mathtt{OffBranch}$ are part of the local notation of the statement but do not occur in this conclusion.
--
--   This is the $q=2$ instance of the uniqueness half of the dictionary between closed points of the two-chart integral model of the level field and places of the reduced Igusa function field: a point lying on the $\infty$-branch, closed in its fibre, avoiding the nonunits of the Gauss ring and with non-supersingular $j$-invariant determines at most one place at which its stalk can be read. It feeds the combined statement [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.reads_unique_and_exists_offBranch_of_not_mem_igusaNodes_twoChartIntegralModel_of_eq_two`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.reads_unique_and_exists_offBranch_of_not_mem_igusaNodes_twoChartIntegralModel_of_eq_two), which pairs this uniqueness with the off-branch clause in the construction of the semistable covering of the modular curve at $q=2$.
